From 4b7d2e1c9a6f3058e2d1c7b9a4f6e8d0c3b5a7f1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Petr=20=C5=A0tetiar?= <petr.stetiar@prplfoundation.org>
Date: Thu, 25 Sep 2026 14:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] AP MLD: leave the first BSS's driver context to
 hostapd_cleanup_driver()

Partially backport 00c2c20d74ee ("hostapd: Maintain single
wpa_driver_nl80211_data (drv) object across interfaces", Aditya Kumar
Singh): retain only the hostapd_free_hapd_data() hunk that leaves the
first BSS's driver context to hostapd_cleanup_driver(). The broader
one-driver-per-wiphy sharing changes and the separate cleanup hunk are
not backported.

On base 5ace39b0, bss[0] can already borrow drv_priv and interface_added
from an MLD partner in hostapd_driver_init(). The partner can be a
secondary BSS created through hostapd_if_add(), so a first BSS can have
interface_added = 1 without the omitted upstream sharing rework.

Before this change, hostapd_free_hapd_data() can remove that first BSS's
registered driver link. If it is the last link on a non-first i802_bss,
the removal frees that i802_bss and clears hapd->drv_priv. However,
hostapd_interface_deinit_free() saved the pointer before per-BSS
teardown and then passes it to hostapd_cleanup_driver(). The sharing
check can therefore read a freed object.

U39 crash 2 (run-37c 102-apmld_step_2) is consistent with this path:
the guest MLD's last 2.4 GHz link is removed while the private MLD's
links on three interfaces named wlan0.1 are still in COUNTRY_UPDATE.
The log includes:

  nl80211: deinit ifname=wlan2.2

The subsequent HT scan fails with ENODEV, beacon setup fails, and the
crash is in i802_sta_deauth() (segfault at 4, hostapd+0x5f9ac). The
allocation/free chain below is a reconstruction from source and logs;
it has not been established by tracing the pointer lifetimes.

If the stale sharing check answers "not shared", hapd_deinit() can
tear down wpa_driver_nl80211_data while other BSSes still use it.
wpa_driver_nl80211_deinit() frees drv->first_bss and drv; it does not
iterate over and free every chained i802_bss. A surviving secondary
BSS can retain a dangling bss->drv. If the private MLD used the freed
first BSS, its hapd->drv_priv can dangle, consistent with the later
bss->flink access in the reconstructed U39 crash chain.

Leave bss[0]'s context live until interface-level driver cleanup. In
the borrowed-context case with other BSSes still on the driver,
is_drv_shared() then sees the live membership and removes only the
departing link/context. When no users remain, driver cleanup performs
the final deinit. Secondary BSSes retain their existing early cleanup.
This does not establish safety for mixed non-ML owners and MLD
borrowers or repair all late setup-failure paths.

After an asynchronous setup failure, the unwind can call
hostapd_free_hapd_data() without interface-level driver cleanup. Such a
bss[0] now retains drv_priv, so plain ENABLE reports "already enabled".
DISABLE followed by ENABLE, REMOVE/ADD, or process restart can perform
the missing cleanup and allow recovery.

Signed-off-by: Petr Štetiar <petr.stetiar@prplfoundation.org>
---
 src/ap/hostapd.c | 5 ++++-
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/ap/hostapd.c b/src/ap/hostapd.c
--- a/src/ap/hostapd.c
+++ b/src/ap/hostapd.c
@@ -552,7 +552,10 @@ void hostapd_free_hapd_data(struct hostapd_data *hapd)

 	authsrv_deinit(hapd);

-	if (hapd->interface_added) {
+	/* For single drv, first bss would have interface_added flag set.
+	 * Don't remove interface now. Driver deinit part will take care
+	 */
+	if (hapd->interface_added && hapd->iface->bss[0] != hapd) {
 		hapd->interface_added = 0;
 		if (hostapd_if_remove(hapd, WPA_IF_AP_BSS, hapd->conf->iface)) {
 			wpa_printf(MSG_WARNING,
